.TH WSYS 4
.SH NAME
wsys \- window system, first prototype (processor backend)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wsys
[
.B -s
.I srvname
]
[
.B -i
.I cmd
]
[
.B -x
]
.PP
.B mount /srv/wsys.$user /mnt/wsys
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Wsys
is the window system of the
.B ui
branch, a successor of
.IR rio (1)
in the making.
It draws on the screen
.IR draw (3)
gives it (or in the window of
.BR /dev/winname ),
posts a 9P connection as
.BI /srv/ srvname
(default
.BR wsys.$user )
and serves a tree of windows.
With
.BR -i ,
it runs
.I cmd
with
.IR rc (1)
in a name space of its own with the tree on
.B /mnt/wsys
and
.B $wsys
naming the service;
.B -x
makes it exit when
.I cmd
does.
.PP
A window's pixels are not sent through the files: they are in global
segments of
.IR segment (3)
that the client makes and owns, its buffers.
As
.I wsys
attaches a segment at the address it has everywhere, each window gets a
range of addresses of its own
.RI ( base ,
.IR size );
the client puts its buffers there, and they cannot collide in
.IR wsys .
A buffer's layout is
.B x8r8g8b8
(32 bits a pixel, little-endian: blue, green, red, unused),
a line every
.I stride
bytes.
Once announced, the client may remove the segment's entry in
.BR #g :
the segment lives while either side has it attached.
.TP
.B clone
Opening it makes a window; the open file is then the window's
.BR ctl ,
and the window lives until it is closed.
Ranges of closed windows are given again.
.TP
.IB n /ctl
Reading gives one line of fields:
.I n
.I x0 y0 x1 y1
(the window's content on the screen)
.I w h
.I base size
(the range for its buffers)
.I focus
(1 if it has the focus).
Messages written:
.RS
.TP
.BI rect " x0 y0 x1 y1
moves the window; with a new size it keeps showing its picture (white
beyond it) until a buffer is presented.
.TP
.BI buffer " b seg w h stride
announces buffer
.I b
(0 to 7): segment
.I seg
of
.I w
by
.I h
pixels.
.I Wsys
attaches it; it must lie in the window's range.
To reuse an address, unbuffer first.
.TP
.BI unbuffer " b
.I wsys
detaches buffer
.IR b .
.TP
.BR present " IbP [Ix0 y0 x1 y1P]"
the pixels of the rectangle (in the buffer's coordinates, which are the
window's; all of the buffer that fits without one) go to the screen.
When
.I wsys
is done with the buffer it sends the event
.BI b " bR:"
the client may write it again.
With two or more buffers the client draws the next picture meanwhile.
.TP
.B top
raises the window and gives it the focus.
.TP
.BI label " text
.TP
.B delete
.RE
.TP
.IB n /event
Reading blocks until there are events; each is a line:
.BI m " x y buttons msec
(the mouse, in the window's coordinates, while it is over the window or a button is held),
.BI k " rune
(a key, the rune in hexadecimal),
.BI r " w h
(a new size),
.BI f " focus
(1: the window got the focus, 0: lost it),
.BI b " b
(buffer
.I b
released).
.PP
.I Wsys
composes on the processor through
.IR draw (3):
a background, the windows bottom to top, each with a border (blue with the focus).
Mouse events come from
.BR /dev/mouse ;
a button pressed over a window gives it the focus and raises it.
Keys come from
.B /dev/cons
in raw mode and go to the focused window.
.SH EXAMPLES
A client in C: see
.BR /tests/ui/wsystest.c .
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/cmd/wsys
(Rust;
.BR scripts/build/cargo.sh )
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR rio (1),
.IR draw (3),
.IR segment (3)
.SH BUGS
A prototype: no window management by mouse (moving, sweeping), no labels
shown, no Vulkan backend yet, no
.IR draw (3)
for old programs in its windows.
.I Wsys
must be allowed to attach the clients' segments: the same user, or the
host owner.
With the processor backend a buffer is released as soon as it is
copied.
When
.I wsys
exits the windows are gone; clients cannot announce theirs again yet.
Keys are read from
.BR /dev/cons :
another reader of it (an idle shell on the same console) takes some.
